# user/hamgreet.ad
#
# hamgreet -- THE GRAPHICAL LOGIN. A gdm-shaped greeter for the Linux lane.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR
# ==============
# The owner asked for "a getty like program for multi ttys/login prompts ...
# and a gdm like login interface for the GUI too". The tty half landed first
# (etc/rc.login + /bin/getty + /bin/login + `supervise`). This is the other
# half.
#
# THE PROPERTY THAT MAKES IT A DISPLAY MANAGER AND NOT A LOCK SCREEN
# ==================================================================
# **It runs BEFORE the session exists.** /etc/rc.d/rc.5 starts the compositor,
# and then runs this program IN THE FOREGROUND. PID 1's rc blocks here. Not
# one program of the desktop session -- not hamdesktop, not hampanelscene, not
# a per-distribution Wayland server, not the /etc/rc.de-user pre-warm -- is
# started until this program has authenticated somebody and written the
# session recipe.
#
# That distinction is the same architectural point that made the tty work
# real. user/hamlock.ad is a CURTAIN: it is laid over a session that already
# started, and everything behind it is already running as somebody. This is
# not that. If you kill the compositor while this is up there is nothing
# behind it, because nothing behind it has been started.
#
# HOW IT DECIDES -- AND IT DOES NOT INVENT A SECOND WAY TO CHECK A PASSWORD
# ========================================================================
# Exactly login(1)'s path, verb for verb:
#
#   1. write "user <name>\n" then "pass <secret>\n" to /dev/auth as TWO whole
#      lines (the device parses one command per write; splitting a line across
#      writes yields malformed commands and denial).
#   2. read the answer. Anything but "ok" is a denial and the field clears.
#   3. on "ok", hand the VERIFIED fd to sys_setuid_auth. The fd is the
#      capability. This program never sees a password hash.
#
# There is no second checker here, no /etc/shadow read, and no fallback that
# lets somebody in when /dev/auth cannot be opened. `_try_auth` returns -1 if
# the device is not there and -1 is a denial.
#
# WHY IT TAKES A TYPED USERNAME rather than resolving one
# =======================================================
# user/hamlock.ad -- which is most of a greeter already, and which this file
# borrows its window and /dev/auth wiring from -- resolves the account from
# the CURRENT uid via passwd_lookup_by_uid. That cannot work here for two
# separate reasons, and both are properties of this tree rather than opinions:
#
#   * A greeter started from rc.5 is PID 1's child and runs as uid 0, and THIS
#     TREE'S /etc/passwd HAS NO uid 0 LINE AT ALL (`hostowner` is uid 1). So
#     the lookup does not merely pick the wrong account, it fails outright.
#   * A display manager on a multi-user machine has to be able to ask WHICH
#     account. Deriving it would make the answer a property of the machine
#     rather than of the person sitting at it.
#
# THE SEAM: A LOGIN PROGRAM CONSTRUCTS THE NAMESPACE THE SESSION GETS
# ===================================================================
# etc/rc.login.linux's THE SEAM note states the rule for the tty path, and the
# same rule holds here:
#
#     "the global root should be min as possable, with apps and things living
#      on fileserver very plan 9 shaped. Drivers can live on the real global
#      root, but that should be an underpinning not the thing the user in
#      userland gets to be running cd /"
#
# So this program does not merely say yes. It WRITES THE SESSION RECIPE --
# /var/lib/hamsession.rc, a hamsh fragment naming the authenticated account --
# and rc.5 sources it and starts the session from it. Today that recipe stamps
# HAMNIX_NEWSHELL_USER, which is the SAME seam login uses: a hamsh started
# with it sources /etc/users/<user>.ns (falling back to default.ns) in the
# session's own namespace. When default.ns stops being subtractive and starts
# binding an app-server root, the graphical session gets it through this file
# without this file changing. The machine's root is NOT hardcoded here as what
# a graphical session inherits -- it is simply what the recipe resolves to
# today, and the recipe is the thing to change.
#
# THE RECIPE IS ALSO THE GATE ON THE SESSION, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
# ==================================================================
# NORTH_STAR.md's worst failure shape is a gap that answers something
# success-shaped. The obvious way to write rc.5 is
#
#     /bin/hamgreet
#     <start the session>
#
# and that is EXACTLY the shape that has burned this project: if the greeter
# dies for any reason -- no screen geometry, no compositor, a failed
# newwindow, a bad exec -- the rc falls through and the session starts with
# nobody authenticated. So the session start is guarded on a VARIABLE that
# only this program can set, in a file only this program writes:
#
#     hamsession_ok = 1
#
# rc.5 sets `hamsession_ok = 0` before running us, sources the recipe after
# us, and starts nothing unless the variable came back positive. A greeter
# that crashes leaves a machine with a compositor and no session, which is a
# visible failure, rather than a machine with an unauthenticated desktop.
#
# AND THE RECIPE IS UNLINKED ON ENTRY. /var/lib is on the ROOT PARTITION and
# survives a reboot. Without the unlink below, a machine that authenticated
# once would carry a valid recipe forever and the SECOND boot would start a
# session from LAST boot's answer without asking anybody. That is the whole
# hole, reintroduced by persistence. It is removed first, before the window
# is drawn, so there is no window in which a stale file is live.
#
# THE LIVE MEDIUM DOES NOT GET A GREETER, AND IT SAYS SO
# ======================================================
# The installer medium autologins as `live`; putting a password prompt in
# front of an installer nobody has an account on would make the medium
# unusable. /etc/installer-medium is the tree's own live probe (planted by
# scripts/hamlinux_image.sh, and REMOVED from the target by user/hlinstall.ad,
# which then verifies it is gone). When it is present this program writes the
# recipe naming `live` and returns immediately, loudly. That is an exception
# to "never answer success-shaped", taken deliberately and named on the
# console, and the gate for this work asserts the file is ABSENT on the
# installed machine it measures -- so the greeter it sees is the real one.
#
# THE PROCESS CENSUS, AND WHY THIS PROGRAM TAKES IT
# =================================================
# The claim that matters is an ABSENCE: no program of the session exists
# before somebody authenticates. An absence is the dangerous kind of
# assertion, so it is measured by an instrument that can be shown to see the
# thing when it IS there, and it is taken at the exact moments that matter
# rather than on a clock:
#
#   /var/lib/hamgreet-ps.pre      the instant the greeter's window is up
#   /var/lib/hamgreet-ps.denied   immediately after a REFUSED password
#   /var/lib/hamgreet-ps.ok       immediately after "ok", before the recipe
#
# and `hamgreet -census <path>` runs the SAME lister as a one-shot, so an rc
# can take the after-the-session-started census with byte-identical code. The
# control is then exact: one instrument, four readings, and the last one must
# name the session programs the first three must not.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
# =====================
#   * It does not offer a session chooser, a user list, an on-screen keyboard,
#     accessibility, or "switch user". gdm has all of those; this has a
#     username, a password and a refusal.
#   * It does not survive its own session. It exits after authenticating, the
#     way `login` execs and is gone. Locking a running session is
#     user/hamlock.ad's job and is a different program on purpose.
#   * It does not start the session itself. rc.5 does, from the recipe.

extern def sys_open(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_open_write(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_close(fd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_unlink(path: Ptr[char]) -> int32
extern def sys_chmod(path: Ptr[char], mode: uint32) -> int32
extern def sys_bind(new_path: Ptr[char], old_path: Ptr[char], flag: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_setuid_auth(authfd: int32) -> int32
extern def sys_exit(code: uint64)

from lib.hamui import hamscene_begin, hamscene_fill, hamscene_glyphs, \
    hamscene_stroke, hamscene_commit
from lib.hamscreen import hamscreen_dims_or_fail
from lib.hamsceneloop import hamscene_park
from lib.p9 import p9_listdir
from lib.passwd.passwd import passwd_parse, passwd_lookup_by_name, \
    passwd_entry_home


# --- tiny helpers ---------------------------------------------------
def _slen(s: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    n: uint64 = 0
    while s[n] != 0:
        n = n + 1
    return n


def _writeall(fd: int32, s: Ptr[uint8]):
    sys_write(fd, s, _slen(s))


def _u64_to_dec(v: uint64, out: Ptr[uint8]) -> uint64:
    if v == 0:
        out[0] = 48
        return 1
    digs: Array[24, uint8]
    n: uint64 = 0
    x: uint64 = v
    while x > 0:
        digs[n] = cast[uint8](x % 10) + 48
        x = x / 10
        n = n + 1
    i: uint64 = 0
    while n > 0:
        n = n - 1
        out[i] = digs[n]
        i = i + 1
    return i


# --- THE TRACE ------------------------------------------------------
# A diagnostic written by a program rc.5 started can go nowhere. PID 1's fd 1
# is /dev/console, which follows the LAST `console=` and is therefore the
# SCREEN the compositor is about to cover; and a spawn child's fd 2 is a
# /fd/2 name opened O_RDONLY, so a message sent there disappears outright
# (user/linux-syscalls.c's sys_bind comment records this, and the fix for the
# bare-127 exec diagnostic is to write /dev/console BY NAME for the same
# reason).
#
# So the trace this program keeps is A FILE ON THE ROOT PARTITION, which an
# instrument can read with debugfs after the machine is off, with nothing
# mounted and nothing to ask the running system. There is no append open on
# this lane, so the whole trace is held in memory and rewritten each time --
# it is a few hundred bytes.
trace_buf: Array[4096, uint8]
trace_len: uint64 = 0

def _trace(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and trace_len + 2 < 4096:
        trace_buf[trace_len] = s[i]
        trace_len = trace_len + 1
        i = i + 1
    trace_buf[trace_len] = 10
    trace_len = trace_len + 1
    trace_buf[trace_len] = 0
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamgreet.trace"))
    if fd >= 0:
        sys_write(fd, &trace_buf[0], trace_len)
        sys_close(fd)
    # AND to fd 1 as well, which on an installed machine reaches the console
    # mirror. Two destinations because neither one alone has been reliable.
    sys_write(1, s, _slen(s))
    sys_write(1, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("\n"), 1)


# --- THE PROCESS CENSUS ---------------------------------------------
# /proc, one directory per pid, comm out of <pid>/stat between the parens --
# the same shape user/ps.ad reads, and it is read here rather than by shelling
# out to ps so that the pre-authentication readings happen at the exact moment
# the greeter reaches a state, with no clock between the state and the
# reading.
# THE BUFFER SIZES ARE NOT ARBITRARY AND A SMALL ONE IS A FALSE ABSENCE.
# The first version of this used a 16 KiB output buffer. On the dev host it
# silently truncated a 933-process listing at 16,383 bytes MID-LINE, and every
# process after the cut was missing from a census that reported success. For
# an instrument whose whole job is to say "hamdesktop is NOT here", a
# truncation that drops the tail is indistinguishable from the absence it is
# supposed to prove. So the buffer is large enough for a listing no machine
# this ships on will exceed, AND an overflow is RECORDED IN THE FILE as
# `CENSUS-TRUNCATED`, which the gate fails on rather than reading as absence.
cens_dir:  Array[262144, uint8]
cens_stat: Array[1024, uint8]
cens_path: Array[64, uint8]
cens_out:  Array[262144, uint8]
cens_len:  uint64 = 0
cens_cut:  int32 = 0

def _cens_put(s: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < n:
        if cens_len + 32 >= 262144:
            cens_cut = 1
            return
        cens_out[cens_len] = s[i]
        cens_len = cens_len + 1
        i = i + 1


def _census(outpath: Ptr[char]) -> int32:
    cens_len = 0
    cens_cut = 0
    n: int64 = p9_listdir(cast[Ptr[char]]("/proc"), &cens_dir[0], 262143)
    if n <= 0:
        return 0
    total: uint64 = cast[uint64](n)
    ls: uint64 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    rows: int32 = 0
    while i <= total:
        atend: int32 = 0
        if i == total:
            atend = 1
        if atend == 1 or cens_dir[i] == 10:
            if i > ls:
                # numeric names only -- a pid directory
                if cens_dir[ls] >= 48 and cens_dir[ls] <= 57:
                    p: uint64 = 0
                    pre: Ptr[uint8] = "/proc/"
                    k: uint64 = 0
                    while pre[k] != 0:
                        cens_path[p] = pre[k]
                        p = p + 1
                        k = k + 1
                    j: uint64 = ls
                    while j < i and p + 8 < 64:
                        cens_path[p] = cens_dir[j]
                        p = p + 1
                        j = j + 1
                    suf: Ptr[uint8] = "/stat"
                    k = 0
                    while suf[k] != 0:
                        cens_path[p] = suf[k]
                        p = p + 1
                        k = k + 1
                    cens_path[p] = 0
                    sfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&cens_path[0]))
                    if sfd >= 0:
                        sn: int64 = sys_read(sfd, &cens_stat[0], 1023)
                        sys_close(sfd)
                        if sn > 0:
                            # the pid, then the comm between the parens
                            _cens_put(&cens_dir[ls], i - ls)
                            _cens_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]](" "), 1)
                            a: uint64 = 0
                            lim: uint64 = cast[uint64](sn)
                            while a < lim and cens_stat[a] != 40:
                                a = a + 1
                            a = a + 1
                            b: uint64 = a
                            while b < lim and cens_stat[b] != 41:
                                b = b + 1
                            if b > a:
                                _cens_put(&cens_stat[a], b - a)
                            _cens_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("\n"), 1)
                            rows = rows + 1
            ls = i + 1
        i = i + 1
    ofd: int32 = sys_open_write(outpath)
    if ofd < 0:
        return 0
    sys_write(ofd, &cens_out[0], cens_len)
    if cens_cut != 0:
        _writeall(ofd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("CENSUS-TRUNCATED\n"))
    sys_close(ofd)
    if cens_cut != 0:
        return 0
    return rows


# --- model ----------------------------------------------------------
SCR_W: int64 = 1024
SCR_H: int64 = 768
GLYPH_W: int64 = 8

USER_CAP: uint64 = 64
PW_CAP:   uint64 = 128
user_buf: Array[64, uint8]
user_len: uint64 = 0
pw_buf:   Array[128, uint8]
pw_len:   uint64 = 0
mask_buf: Array[136, uint8]
field:    int32 = 0                # 0 = username, 1 = password
fails:    int32 = 0

_pw_file: Array[8192, uint8]
home_buf: Array[128, uint8]
line_buf: Array[512, uint8]
resp_buf: Array[64, uint8]


def _rebuild_mask():
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < pw_len and i < 128:
        mask_buf[i] = 42                                   # '*'
        i = i + 1
    mask_buf[i] = 0


# The username is rendered as typed. It is not a secret and a person has to be
# able to see they typed it right; the password is masked and is never
# rendered, only counted.
def _uname_cstr() -> Ptr[uint8]:
    user_buf[user_len] = 0
    return &user_buf[0]


# --- render ---------------------------------------------------------
# EVERY STRING HERE IS AN INSTRUMENT AS WELL AS A LABEL. The only way to read
# this screen back is OCR of a screendump, so the words are chosen to be
# unambiguous at 8 px and to be different from each other: a gate that
# confuses "Password" with "Login incorrect" is measuring nothing.
def _paint(wid: int32):
    bw: int64 = 420
    bh: int64 = 220
    bx: int64 = SCR_W / 2 - bw / 2
    by: int64 = SCR_H / 2 - bh / 2

    hamscene_begin()
    hamscene_fill(0, 0, cast[int32](SCR_W), cast[int32](SCR_H),
                  cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#0e1018"))
    hamscene_fill(cast[int32](bx), cast[int32](by),
                  cast[int32](bw), cast[int32](bh),
                  cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#20242e"))
    hamscene_stroke(cast[int32](bx), cast[int32](by),
                    cast[int32](bw), cast[int32](bh), 1,
                    cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#3a4256"))

    title: Ptr[uint8] = "HAMNIX LOGIN"
    tw: int64 = cast[int64](_slen(title)) * GLYPH_W
    hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](SCR_W / 2 - tw / 2),
                    cast[int32](by + 20), title,
                    cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#dce4f5"))

    fx: int64 = bx + 24
    fw: int64 = bw - 48

    # --- username -------------------------------------------------
    hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](by + 52),
                    cast[Ptr[uint8]]("Username"),
                    cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#9aa6c0"))
    uy: int64 = by + 68
    hamscene_fill(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](uy),
                  cast[int32](fw), 26, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#12151d"))
    ucol: Ptr[uint8] = "#4a5468"
    if field == 0:
        ucol = "#7fa8ff"
    hamscene_stroke(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](uy),
                    cast[int32](fw), 26, 1, ucol)
    hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](fx + 6), cast[int32](uy + 9),
                    _uname_cstr(), cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#c8d7f5"))

    # --- password -------------------------------------------------
    hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](by + 110),
                    cast[Ptr[uint8]]("Password"),
                    cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#9aa6c0"))
    py: int64 = by + 126
    hamscene_fill(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](py),
                  cast[int32](fw), 26, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#12151d"))
    pcol: Ptr[uint8] = "#4a5468"
    if field == 1:
        pcol = "#7fa8ff"
    hamscene_stroke(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](py),
                    cast[int32](fw), 26, 1, pcol)
    _rebuild_mask()
    hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](fx + 6), cast[int32](py + 9),
                    &mask_buf[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#c8d7f5"))

    # --- the status line ------------------------------------------
    sy: int64 = by + 178
    if fails > 0:
        hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](sy),
                        cast[Ptr[uint8]]("Login incorrect"),
                        cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#e07070"))
    else:
        hamscene_glyphs(cast[int32](fx), cast[int32](sy),
                        cast[Ptr[uint8]]("Type your name and password"),
                        cast[Ptr[uint8]]("#6b7690"))
    hamscene_commit(wid)


# --- /dev/auth ------------------------------------------------------
# Framing matters: the device parses ONE command per write, so a line split
# across two writes is a malformed command and a denial. login.ad frames it
# the same way and says so.
def _auth_line(fd: int32, prefix: Ptr[uint8], payload: Ptr[uint8],
               paylen: uint64):
    p: uint64 = 0
    while prefix[p] != 0 and p + 2 < 512:
        line_buf[p] = prefix[p]
        p = p + 1
    j: uint64 = 0
    while j < paylen and p + 2 < 512:
        line_buf[p] = payload[j]
        p = p + 1
        j = j + 1
    line_buf[p] = 10
    p = p + 1
    sys_write(fd, &line_buf[0], p)


# Returns the VERIFIED /dev/auth fd on success, or -1. The fd is the
# capability sys_setuid_auth consumes; it is deliberately NOT closed here.
def _try_auth() -> int32:
    afd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/auth"))
    if afd < 0:
        # A greeter that cannot reach the checker must REFUSE, not admit.
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: /dev/auth did not open -- refusing"))
        return -1
    _auth_line(afd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("user "), _uname_cstr(), user_len)
    _auth_line(afd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("pass "), &pw_buf[0], pw_len)
    rn: int64 = sys_read(afd, &resp_buf[0], 63)
    if rn >= 2 and resp_buf[0] == 111 and resp_buf[1] == 107:      # "ok"
        return afd
    sys_close(afd)
    return -1


# --- the session recipe ---------------------------------------------
# A hamsh fragment. Two statements per variable, never `export NAME=VALUE` --
# hamsh's tokenizer splits on '=' and the stray '=' is a parse error that
# would abort the rest of rc.5, so the session would never start at all. The
# value is single-quoted because an unquoted assignment RHS is parsed as an
# expression (a bare /home/x reads '/' as divide). Both traps are recorded in
# etc/rc.de-user.linux and both are load-bearing here.
recipe_buf: Array[1024, uint8]
recipe_len: uint64 = 0

def _rec_put(s: Ptr[uint8]):
    i: uint64 = 0
    while s[i] != 0 and recipe_len + 1 < 1024:
        recipe_buf[recipe_len] = s[i]
        recipe_len = recipe_len + 1
        i = i + 1


def _write_recipe(name: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    recipe_len = 0
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("# /var/lib/hamsession.rc -- WRITTEN BY /bin/hamgreet.\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("# This file is the ONLY thing that authorises a graphical session on\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("# this machine. /etc/rc.d/rc.5 sets hamsession_ok = 0, runs the greeter,\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("# sources this, and starts nothing unless hamsession_ok came back > 0.\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("# hamgreet unlinks it on entry, so it never outlives the boot that\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("# earned it.\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamsession_ok = 1\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamsession_user='"))
    _rec_put(name)
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("'\n"))
    # THE SEAM. hamsh sources /etc/users/<user>.ns for a session stamped with
    # this, exactly as `login` arranges for a tty session. When that file
    # stops subtracting from the machine's root and starts binding an app
    # server, the graphical session gets it here without this file changing.
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("HAMNIX_NEWSHELL_USER='"))
    _rec_put(name)
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("'\n"))
    _rec_put(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("export HAMNIX_NEWSHELL_USER\n"))
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamsession.rc"))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    sys_write(fd, &recipe_buf[0], recipe_len)
    sys_close(fd)
    sys_chmod(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamsession.rc"), 384)      # 0600
    return 1


def _load_passwd():
    fd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/etc/passwd"))
    if fd < 0:
        return
    total: uint64 = 0
    while total < 8192:
        n: int64 = sys_read(fd, &_pw_file[total], 8192 - total)
        if n <= 0:
            break
        total = total + cast[uint64](n)
    sys_close(fd)
    passwd_parse(&_pw_file[0], total)


# --- THE SESSION MARKER ---------------------------------------------
# Written AFTER sys_setuid_auth, in the authenticated account's own home, so
# its owner on the ext4 IS the uid the verified fd resolved to. Read back with
# debugfs on an unmounted partition, that is the answer to "whose session is
# this?" taken from the disk rather than from the machine's own opinion of
# itself. If the drop had silently not happened the file would be root-owned;
# if the fd had named the wrong account it would be in the wrong home.
def _write_session_marker() -> int32:
    p: uint64 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while home_buf[i] != 0 and p + 24 < 128:
        line_buf[p] = home_buf[i]
        p = p + 1
        i = i + 1
    suf: Ptr[uint8] = "/.hamsession"
    k: uint64 = 0
    while suf[k] != 0:
        line_buf[p] = suf[k]
        p = p + 1
        k = k + 1
    line_buf[p] = 0
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&line_buf[0]))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    _writeall(fd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("graphical session opened by "))
    _writeall(fd, _uname_cstr())
    _writeall(fd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("\n"))
    sys_close(fd)
    return 1


def _is_live_medium() -> int32:
    fd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/etc/installer-medium"))
    if fd < 0:
        return 0
    sys_close(fd)
    return 1


def _streq(a: Ptr[uint8], b: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    i: uint64 = 0
    while a[i] != 0 and b[i] != 0:
        if a[i] != b[i]:
            return 0
        i = i + 1
    if a[i] == b[i]:
        return 1
    return 0


def _winpath(out: Ptr[uint8], wid: int32, leaf: Ptr[uint8]):
    p: uint64 = 0
    pre: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]]("/dev/wsys/")
    j: uint64 = 0
    while pre[j] != 0:
        out[p] = pre[j]
        p = p + 1
        j = j + 1
    p = p + _u64_to_dec(cast[uint64](wid), &out[p])
    k: uint64 = 0
    while leaf[k] != 0:
        out[p] = leaf[k]
        p = p + 1
        k = k + 1
    out[p] = 0


def _newwindow() -> int32:
    wfd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/wsys/ctl"))
    if wfd < 0:
        return -1
    sys_write(wfd, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("newwindow\n"), 10)
    sys_close(wfd)
    rfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev/wsys/ctl"))
    if rfd < 0:
        return -1
    rbuf: Array[32, uint8]
    r: int64 = sys_read(rfd, &rbuf[0], 31)
    sys_close(rfd)
    if r <= 0:
        return -1
    wid: int32 = 0
    i: uint64 = 0
    while i < cast[uint64](r):
        c: uint8 = rbuf[i]
        if c >= 48 and c <= 57:
            wid = wid * 10 + cast[int32](c) - 48
        i = i + 1
    return wid


def _ctl(path: Ptr[uint8], verb: Ptr[uint8]):
    fd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](path))
    if fd >= 0:
        _writeall(fd, verb)
        sys_close(fd)


# --- authentication, and everything that follows it -----------------
# Returns 1 when a session has been authorised (and the caller exits), 0 on a
# denial (and the caller keeps the greeter up).
def _attempt(wid: int32, ctlp: Ptr[uint8]) -> int32:
    afd: int32 = _try_auth()
    if afd < 0:
        fails = fails + 1
        # BOTH fields clear and the caret goes back to the name, which is what
        # `login` does (it reprompts from "login: ", not from "Password: ")
        # and what gdm does (it returns to the user list). It also means a
        # denial leaves NO typed state behind for the next person at the
        # machine, and that the retry path is the same keystroke sequence as
        # the first attempt -- which is what lets one drive exercise a junk
        # account, a wrong password and the right one without ever having to
        # count backspaces.
        pw_len = 0
        user_len = 0
        user_buf[0] = 0
        field = 0
        _census(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamgreet-ps.denied"))
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: DENIED"))
        return 0
    _census(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamgreet-ps.ok"))
    _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: authenticated"))

    # Resolve the account BEFORE dropping: /etc/passwd is world-readable, but
    # doing it in one order every time is one less thing to reason about.
    _load_passwd()
    idx: int32 = passwd_lookup_by_name(_uname_cstr())
    home_buf[0] = 0
    if idx >= 0:
        h: Ptr[uint8] = passwd_entry_home(idx)
        i: uint64 = 0
        while h[i] != 0 and i + 1 < 128:
            home_buf[i] = h[i]
            i = i + 1
        home_buf[i] = 0

    # The recipe is written while still root: /var/lib is 0755 root-owned and
    # the session it authorises is not the writer's to grant afterwards.
    if _write_recipe(_uname_cstr()) == 0:
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: FAILED to write the session recipe -- no session will start"))

    # Take the curtain down before dropping, so a failure below cannot leave a
    # full-screen window over a session nobody can reach.
    _ctl(ctlp, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("close\n"))

    su: int32 = sys_setuid_auth(afd)
    sys_close(afd)
    if su < 0:
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: identity change FAILED after ok"))
        return 1
    if home_buf[0] != 0:
        _write_session_marker()
    return 1


def main(argc: int32, argv: Ptr[uint64]) -> int32:
    # `hamgreet -census <path>` -- the one-shot lister. This exists so that the
    # AFTER-the-session census is taken by byte-identical code to the three
    # before it: a control on the instrument is only a control if it is the
    # same instrument.
    if argc >= 3:
        flag: Ptr[uint8] = cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[1])
        if _streq(flag, "-census") == 1:
            r: int32 = _census(cast[Ptr[char]](cast[Ptr[uint8]](argv[2])))
            if r <= 0:
                _writeall(2, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: census wrote nothing\n"))
                return 1
            return 0

    sys_bind(cast[Ptr[char]]("/dev"), cast[Ptr[char]]("#c"), 0)

    # FIRST, before anything can go wrong and before a window exists: a recipe
    # from a previous boot must not authorise this one. See the header.
    sys_unlink(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamsession.rc"))

    if _is_live_medium() != 0:
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: /etc/installer-medium is present -- this is the INSTALL MEDIUM, which autologins as live. NO PASSWORD IS ASKED FOR ON THIS BOOT."))
        _write_recipe(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("live"))
        return 0

    wdim: Array[1, int64]
    hdim: Array[1, int64]
    # Refusing to present is the safe direction: rc.5's guard means no recipe
    # is written, so no session starts, and the machine says why on the
    # console rather than handing out a desktop.
    if hamscreen_dims_or_fail(&wdim[0], &hdim[0],
                              cast[Ptr[uint8]]("[hamgreet]")) == 0:
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: no screen geometry -- NO SESSION WILL BE STARTED"))
        return 1
    SCR_W = wdim[0]
    SCR_H = hdim[0]

    wid: int32 = _newwindow()
    if wid < 0:
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: newwindow FAILED -- NO SESSION WILL BE STARTED"))
        return 1

    ctlp:  Array[64, uint8]
    keysp: Array[64, uint8]
    _winpath(&ctlp[0], wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("/ctl"))
    _winpath(&keysp[0], wid, cast[Ptr[uint8]]("/keys"))

    gbuf: Array[64, uint8]
    gp: uint64 = 0
    pre: Ptr[uint8] = "geometry 0 0 "
    kk: uint64 = 0
    while pre[kk] != 0:
        gbuf[gp] = pre[kk]
        gp = gp + 1
        kk = kk + 1
    gp = gp + _u64_to_dec(cast[uint64](SCR_W), &gbuf[gp])
    gbuf[gp] = 32
    gp = gp + 1
    gp = gp + _u64_to_dec(cast[uint64](SCR_H), &gbuf[gp])
    gbuf[gp] = 10
    gp = gp + 1
    cfd: int32 = sys_open_write(cast[Ptr[char]](&ctlp[0]))
    if cfd >= 0:
        sys_write(cfd, &gbuf[0], gp)
        sys_close(cfd)
    _ctl(&ctlp[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("decorate 0\n"))
    _ctl(&ctlp[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 300\n"))
    _paint(wid)
    # Re-assert z AFTER the first commit's raise+focus. There is nothing else
    # on the screen yet -- that is the whole point of this program -- but the
    # compositor's own chrome and any later client must stay under it.
    _ctl(&ctlp[0], cast[Ptr[uint8]]("z 300\n"))

    _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: the graphical login is presenting"))
    _census(cast[Ptr[char]]("/var/lib/hamgreet-ps.pre"))

    kfd: int32 = sys_open(cast[Ptr[char]](&keysp[0]))
    if kfd < 0:
        _trace(cast[Ptr[uint8]]("hamgreet: cannot open the key ring -- NO SESSION WILL BE STARTED"))
        return 1

    kbuf: Array[256, uint8]
    while True:
        r: int64 = sys_read(kfd, &kbuf[0], 255)
        if r <= 0:
            # /keys is waitfds-wake-wired, so the first character wakes this
            # immediately; the 200 ms cap is the backstop for a kernel that
            # delivered without notifying. NOT a yield spin -- user/hamlock.ad
            # records what that cost (14.4 s of cpu in a 15.8 s run).
            hamscene_park(kfd, 200)
            continue
        changed: int32 = 0
        i: uint64 = 0
        while i < cast[uint64](r):
            if kbuf[i] == 100:                             # 'd', a press
                kj: uint64 = i + 1
                while kj < cast[uint64](r) and kbuf[kj] == 32:
                    kj = kj + 1
                code: int32 = 0
                got: int32 = 0
                while kj < cast[uint64](r) and kbuf[kj] >= 48 \
                      and kbuf[kj] <= 57:
                    code = code * 10 + cast[int32](kbuf[kj]) - 48
                    kj = kj + 1
                    got = 1
                if got != 0:
                    if code == 9:                          # Tab: switch field
                        if field == 0:
                            field = 1
                        else:
                            field = 0
                        changed = 1
                    elif code == 10 or code == 13:         # Enter
                        if field == 0:
                            # Enter on the name moves to the password, the way
                            # every login prompt in the world does. It is also
                            # what lets a drive reach the password field
                            # without depending on Tab.
                            field = 1
                            changed = 1
                        elif user_len > 0:
                            if _attempt(wid, &ctlp[0]) != 0:
                                sys_exit(0)
                            changed = 1
                        else:
                            field = 0
                            changed = 1
                    elif code == 8 or code == 127:         # Backspace
                        if field == 0:
                            if user_len > 0:
                                user_len = user_len - 1
                                changed = 1
                        else:
                            if pw_len > 0:
                                pw_len = pw_len - 1
                                changed = 1
                    elif code == 27:
                        # Escape is IGNORED. There is nothing behind this
                        # window to be dismissed to.
                        changed = 0
                    elif code >= 32 and code < 127:
                        if field == 0:
                            if user_len < USER_CAP - 1:
                                user_buf[user_len] = cast[uint8](code)
                                user_len = user_len + 1
                                changed = 1
                        else:
                            if pw_len < PW_CAP - 1:
                                pw_buf[pw_len] = cast[uint8](code)
                                pw_len = pw_len + 1
                                changed = 1
                i = kj
            else:
                i = i + 1
        if changed != 0:
            _paint(wid)
    return 0
